    "reading_order": 7,
    "text": "A quadratic equation in the variable x is an equation of the form ax2 + bx + c = 0, where\na, b, c are real numbers, a  0. For example, 2x2 + x – 300 = 0 is a quadratic equation.\nSimilarly, 2x2 – 3x + 1 = 0, 4x – 3x2 + 2 = 0 and 1 – x2 + 300 = 0 are also quadratic\nequations.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 2,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p002_b08",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      302.62,
      420.01,
      357.69
    ],
    "reading_order": 8,
    "text": "In fact, any equation of the form p(x) = 0, where p(x) is a polynomial of degree\n2, is a quadratic equation. But when we write the terms of p(x) in descending order of\ntheir degrees, then we get the standard form of the equation. That is, ax2 + bx + c = 0,\na  0 is called the standard form of a quadratic equation.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
